    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-2",
      "name": "SciKit-Learn Experiments",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "A series of AI-Generated python tasks to test which features of scikit-learn do or don't work with our implementation of PythonLab.\n\nHow these were generated:\n- Used Gemini CLI in a directory that had a CSV of the CIW AI standards and and [docs folder from the Scikit-learn github.](https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/tree/main/doc)\n- Gemini was prompted to analyze the standards and analyze the docs and make a plan for creating examples with exemplars that mapped from the standards to scikit-learn tools. Examples should follow a \"worked example\" model, where the code demonstrates/explains a concept, and then there is a prompt to edit/remix/modify the code in a new context as a task for a student\n- The initial list missed some standards and I realized I needed to explicitly say \"it's okay if an example doesn't use scikit-learn at all, and instead only uses pandas or numpy\". This helped catch several data standards\n- I manually prompted a few other standards I wanted it to try and cover\n- Once the examples were generated, I moved them into levels. AI took ~15 mins to generate these; I took 2 hours manually moving into levelbuilder.\n\nThese examples are NOT meant to represent \"good pedagogy\" or potential student-facing activities. They're purely a way to \"stress test\" potential alignment between scikit-learn and the CIW standards, and then see if PythonLab can run the tasks associated with them"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-2",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "cs50-testing-things"
      }
    }
  ],
  "lesson_activities": [
    {
      "key": "08774b80-963d-4d4a-9936-edcf3170193a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
